### docs/FIXES_AND_LEARNINGS.md — Critical Failures Log

This file documents every significant bug, failure, or architectural decision encountered
during the build — along with exactly how it was fixed.

This is a personal interview reference. Write it as if explaining to a future interviewer
who asks "tell me about a challenge you faced on this project."

Update it every time something breaks and gets fixed. Do not skip minor-seeming bugs —
they are often the most interesting to discuss.

Structure for each entry:
```
## [Short title of the issue]
**Date:** [when it happened]
**Phase:** [which phase of the build]
**Severity:** Critical / High / Medium

### What Happened
[1-2 sentences: what broke and how it was discovered]

### Root Cause
[Technical explanation of why it happened]

### How It Was Fixed
[Exact fix — code snippet if applicable]

### What I Learned
[Key takeaway — architectural insight, library gotcha, debugging technique]

### Relevant for Interview
[Why this is worth discussing: system design, debugging, trade-off reasoning, etc.]
```

Pre-populate with this first entry as a template:
```
## Initial Entry — Log Created
**Date:** [project start date]
**Phase:** Phase 1

### What Happened
Project initialized. This log will track all critical failures and fixes.

### Root Cause
N/A

### How It Was Fixed
N/A

### What I Learned
Documenting failures in real-time is far more accurate than reconstructing from memory.

### Relevant for Interview
Shows disciplined engineering habits and self-awareness about failure modes.
```

